<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Krishnadevaraya</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Krishna Deva Raya, Krishnadeva Raya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Krishnadevaraya was emperor of Vijayanagara from 1509 to 1529, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuluva-dynasty">Tuluva dynasty</a>, and the most celebrated ruler of the empire. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>, took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> in 1520 and conquered coastal Andhra from the Gajapatis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. A poet in Telugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, he patronised the poets known as the Ashtadiggajas and built widely at Hampi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya.jpg" alt="Krishnadevaraya" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravitheja Kumar Reddy C · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinnadevi,_Krishnadevaraya,_Tirumaladevi_statues_at_Chandragiri_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Barid family kept the remaining Bahmani sultans as puppets at Bidar, while <b>Krishnadevaraya</b> of Vijayanagara exploited the disorder to take the Raichur Doab in 1520.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest commander was <b>Krishnadevaraya</b> (r. 1509–1529), who defeated the Gajapati rulers of Odisha in campaigns between 1513 and 1519, taking Udayagiri and Kondavidu, and in 1520 defeated Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur at the Battle of Raichur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Krishnadevaraya</b> of Vijayanagara took the southern provinces from Prataparudra, and the Golconda Sultanate pressed from the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/gajapati-kingdom">Gajapati Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hampi, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Hampi, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.34,76.46&amp;year=1519" aria-label="Hampi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6638 1593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Krishnadevaraya"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7088 1863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7088 1863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.34,76.46&amp;year=1519">What was here in 1519? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
